
The Python Imaging Library
$Id: README,v 1.9 1997/01/14 18:01:06 fl Exp fl $

Release 0.2b4 (January 10, 1997)

====================================================================
The Python Imaging Library 0.2b4
====================================================================

This is a beta release of the Python Imaging Library (PIL).  Many
features planned for 0.2 are still not being distributed, and some of
the new stuff in this release may not work as expected.

News highlights:

+ Plugged a number of serious memory leaks; PIL leaked lots of
  memory when loading PNG and tiled/striped TIFF images, and
  even more when saving images in *any* format.

+ PIL now reads nearly all formats that previous versions could
  only identify.  This includes FLI/FLC, MSP, PSD, TIFF w. pre-
  dictor, TGA, and XPM.  Decoders for compressed versions of SUN
  and TGA have also been added, but they are completely untested
  (sample files would be much appreciated).

+ PIL now writes BMP, uncompressed GIF, uncompressed MSP, and PNG.

+ PIL now handles ARG, FLI, and GIF animations.  Use the "player"
  script to view an animation.  The "gifmaker" script can be
  used to create GIF animations.

+ PIL now allows you to control the quality when writing JPEG
  files.  You can also write progressive JPEG files.

+ The "image2py" script converts an image to a Python module.

For more information on these and other changes, see the CHANGES
document.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE: This version of PIL has only been tested with Python 1.4.  It
is likely to work with 1.3 under Unix, but to build the Windows
version using the provided makefiles, you'll need Python 1.4 final
and Visual C++ 4.0 or later.
--------------------------------------------------------------------

For installation and licensing details, see below.

For questions, comments and bug reports, send mail to
'image-sig@python.org'.


--------------------------------------------------------------------
Build instructions for UNIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------

NOTE: To build the Python Imaging Library, you need an ANSI C
compiler.  The "cc" compiler on SunOS 4 is not ANSI compliant.  On
that platform, use "acc" or "gcc" instead.

1. If you need JPEG and/or PNG support, make sure to get and build
   the necessary external libraries:

   - for JPEG support, get the IJG JPEG library, version 6 (version
     6a is fine, but require some minor modifications to the inter-
     face.  See the IJG changelog for details).

   - for PNG and ZIP support, get the ZLIB library (by Gailly/Adler).
     PIL was tested with version 1.0.4, but is likely to work with
     versions from 0.95 and onwards.


2. Unpack the PIL distribution (the file Imaging-0.2b1.tar.gz) in your
   Python/Extensions directory.

	$ cd Python-1.4/Extensions (example)
	$ gunzip Imaging-0.2b1.tar.gz
	$ tar xvf Imaging-0.2b1.tar


3. Now build the Imaging core library (libImaging.a).  Type:

	$ cd Imaging/libImaging
	$ ./configure
	$ make


4. To check that this library works as it should, type:

	$ make check

   It isn't much of a test, but it shouldn't crash, at least :-)


5. Go back to the Imaging directory.

   If you don't have the IJG JPEG or ZLIB libraries, or wish to install
   Python in a non-standard place, copy the "Setup.in" file to "Setup"
   and edit it according to the instructions in it.


6. Edit Makefile.pre.in and build a Makefile following the instructions
   there.  In most cases, you can simply give the following command:

	$ make -f Makefile.pre.in boot


7. To build a dynamically loaded module, just type "make":

	$ make

   This will create a file called "_imagingmodule.so".

   Then type (assuming a standard shell):

	$ cd test
	$ PYTHONPATH=..:../Lib ; export PYTHONPATH
	$ python

	>>> import _imaging
	>>> import Image

   If both imports works, you've successfully added PIL to your Python
   environment.  There's a few demo scripts in the Imaging/Scripts
   directory that you can use to further test the library.


8. Copy the "_imagingmodule.so" module and the contents of the
   "Imaging/Lib" directory to a suitable place along the Python path.


* Adding Tkinter support

1. Add the following lines to tkappinit.c (part of the standard
   Python distribution), after the MOREBUTTONS stuff:

	{
	    extern void TkImaging_Init(void);
	    TkImaging_Init();
	}

   This registers a image type called "PyImagingCore", which is
   use to communicate between PIL and Tk's PhotoImage handler.

2. Compile Python's _tkinter.c with the WITH_APPINIT flag set
   (see the Setup file for details).

3. Link the _tkinter module with _tkinter.c and tkappinit.c (you
   have to edit Python's Setup file to get this effect).


--------------------------------------------------------------------
Build instructions for Windows
--------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE: If possible, use the prebuilt PIL distribution for Windows.
It includes the IJG JPEG and ZLIB libraries, and has been tested
with Python 1.4 as well as with Mark Hammond's PythonWin.  To build
the library yourself, you need Visual C++ 4.0 or later, and a locally
compiled version of the 1.4 distribution.
--------------------------------------------------------------------

PIL is shipped with a Visual C++ project makefile.  It contains
subprojects for the IJG JPEG and ZLIB libraries as well.  Unless
you're really lucky, you'll probably have to tweak the makefile
somewhat to fit your configuration.


1. Unpack the PIL distribution (the file Imaging-0.2b1.tar.gz) in
   a suitable directory.  The provided makefile assumes PIL to
   be located in a sibling to Python's own 'PC' directory.

   Also unpack the IJG JPEG and ZLIB libraries, and place their
   directories in the Imaging subdirectory (as jpeg-6 and zlib,
   respectively).

   Note that the distribution is a gzipped archive.  You can use
   WinZip to unpack the distribution.


2. Build the module from within the developer's studio, or by giving
   the following command:

	$ nmake -f _imaging.mak


3. Copy the "_imaging.dll" file and the contents of the Lib directory
   to a suitable place along your Python path.


* Adding Tkinter support

See the Unix section.  Instead of modifying the Setup file, you have
to add tkappinit.c to the project file, and -DWITH_APPINIT to the
_tkinter.c compile options.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE: If you use Tk 4.2, you may wish to install the booster patch
which provides dramatic performance improvements for 16- and 24-bit
displays.  See the description in the Tk subdirectory for details.
--------------------------------------------------------------------


